    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d824">

                <pb facs="0982" n="972" sameAs="frus:frus1944v02/pg972"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.6176/7–1244: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-07-12T19:00:00+02:00">July 12, 1944—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 2—3:06 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5490. Reference Embassy’s 5103.<anchor n="34" xml:id="d824-f1"/> Complete British
                    representation still undecided. However unofficially understand following seven
                        <pb facs="0983" n="973" xml:id="pg973"/> delegates, Franks, Chairman,
                    Clauson, Fennelly, Beharrell,<anchor n="35" xml:id="d824-f2"/> Hay, Figg, one
                    unnamed [from] Foreign Office plus four advisers, Bail-lieu,<anchor n="36"
                        xml:id="d824-f3"/> and one each unnamed from following: Board of Trade,
                    Treasury, Industry, plus Secretariat Hall,<anchor n="37" xml:id="d824-f4"/> A.
                    G. Pawson.<anchor n="38" xml:id="d824-f5"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[A press release issued by the Department of State on July 18, 1944,
                        announced the Department’s acceptance of the British invitation to engage in
                        conversations on postwar rubber problems and listed the advisers who would
                        accompany Mr. Lockwood and Mr. Phillips to London; for text of press
                        release, see Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, July
                        23, 1944, page 84.]</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d824-f1" n="34" xml:id="d824-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram of June 28, 5 p.m., not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d824-f2" n="35" xml:id="d824-f2-content">
                <p>Sir George Beharrell, Chairman, Dunlop Rubber Company.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d824-f3" n="36" xml:id="d824-f3-content">
                <p>Sir Clive L. Baillieu, Director, Dunlop Rubber Company; Head of British Raw
                    Materials Mission at Washington, 1942–1943.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d824-f4" n="37" xml:id="d824-f4-content">
                <p>R. L. Hall, Assistant Secretary, British Ministry of Supply.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d824-f5" n="38" xml:id="d824-f5-content">
                <p>A. G. Pawson, British Colonial Office.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
